title: 'ITIL 4, change enablement: who approves a change is set by its risk; low-risk, repeatable changes are pre-authorized, and not every change goes to one board.'
pid: c96d488a-8616-40bb-81aa-ced024b334fd
body: |2-
  https://itsm.tools/change-enablement/
  Read in a summary by a vendor, not in ITIL's own text, which is
  sold. Normal changes are assessed by "urgency, complexity, and
  impact"; the authority may be a peer, an automated rule, or the
  business owner for high risk. The page gives no method for
  assessing risk. This is the root of step 4's tiers.
